  NO .proto changed. NO existing .cddl changed. NO reason code added, renamed or
  reclassified. The other THREE registries are untouched: reason_codes stays at
  4, capabilities and encrypted_resolvers stay at 2. contracts/gen/** is
  BYTE-IDENTICAL — a .cddl generates no binding.

WHY IT IS A DEFECT AND NOT AN INCONVENIENCE (§3 step 1):
  This is W-21, open since wave 1. `PairingOffer` is named normatively by
  architecture.md S-67, ADR-0007 §7.4, ADR-0017 §11.3 and §11.9, ADR-0020 S-67
  and ADR-0023 §7.6 / EM-21..EM-26 — and it exists in the corpus ONLY as a
  ten-line pseudo-CDDL block inside ADR-0007 §7.4. No .cddl, no .proto, no
  registry entry, no Rust type, and not one bound in limits.json that fits any
  of its seven fields.

  The consequence is not cosmetic and it is not deferrable: C-B is the channel
  authenticator for EVERY enrolment path. ADR-0023 EM-21 records that headless
  and cameraless devices use C-B too rather than falling back to C-A's ~2^29.9,
  and W-22 records that C-A is unimplementable — no audited RFC 9382 P-256 Rust
  implementation exists. So with C-B uncontracted, NEITHER channel authenticator
  is complete, and enrolment — the operation every other operation depends on —
  cannot be implemented against a contract because there is no contract.

WHY IT IS A .cddl AND NOT A .proto (§3 step 4):
  pairing.proto's own header states the SECRET-FIELD PROHIBITION: "pairing_secret,
  the SPAKE2 password, K_pair, and PairSecret NEVER appear in this schema and
  MUST NOT be added". Field 1 of this payload IS pairing_secret. That file's
  messages are C1/C2 WIRE messages, and the whole security argument of C-B is
  that pairing_secret NEVER TRANSITS THE NETWORK (§7.4: "MITM at the rendezvous
  is defeated by construction: the adversary never sees pairing_secret"). A
  protobuf definition invites exactly one mistake — embedding it in a
  PairingRequest — and that mistake hands the rendezvous the one value it must
  never see.

  Nor signed_statements.cddl: every member of that file is COSE_Sign1-wrapped
  and VERIFIED, and the joining device is by definition not yet enrolled, holds
  no OwnerTrustAnchor and no TrustedPeer, and has no key to verify against. A
  COSE_Sign1 nobody can check is bytes shaped like a proof. Its inventory is
  also at 17 of the ~20 that fire ADR-0003 §14 revisit trigger 7.

WHY IT IS COMPATIBLE (§3 step 5):
  Additive in the strongest sense available: a NEW file that no existing
  producer or consumer reads, and six NEW keys in an object that already exists.
  No field number, no enum value, no wire format, no reason code and no existing
  bound moved. No generated binding regenerates, because CDDL has no codegen in
  this repository. The one wire-visible consequence is
  SchemaDescriptor.schema_digest, which moves exactly as it did for Amendments
  1, 2 and 3 — that is what the field is for.

THE BOUNDS ARE MEASURED, NOT ESTIMATED (§3 step 2):
  A minimal RFC 8949 §4.2.1 encoder was used on a representative offer: a COSE_Key
  EC2 P-256 compressed key (43 B), a tunnel-key-binding built exactly as
  signed_statements.cddl §2 declares it (142 B) inside a COSE_Sign1 (216 B), and
  a 27-character hint. Total 377 bytes. The per-field bounds sum to 493 at their
  maxima, at or below max_offer_bytes = 512, and the payload cap is checked
  BEFORE any field is parsed so the two cannot disagree in the dangerous
  direction. That relation is asserted by a contract test rather than hoped for —
  Amendment 1's recorded cost was exactly a per-field cap exceeding its envelope
  cap and passing because nothing compared them.

WHAT THIS AMENDMENT NARROWS, STATED PLAINLY:
  ADR-0007 §7.4 writes `attestation : bstr / null`. max_offer_attestation_bytes
  is 0, so `null` is the only admissible value, and THAT IS A NARROWING OF THE
  ADR. The reason is measured and is finding F-1: the offer is already 377 bytes
  with attestation absent, and ADR-0023 EM-22 E1's declared 71-column terminal
  admits a QR of at most 61 modules with a conforming 4-module quiet zone —
  version 11, 321 bytes at EC level L. The product's DEFAULT enrolment channel
  is 56 bytes too small for its own payload before any attestation blob, at the
  weakest error-correction level, for a symbol to be photographed off a glowing
  screen. No document in the corpus fixes the QR version, the error-correction
  level or the quiet zone.

  THIS AMENDMENT DOES NOT RESOLVE F-1 AND MUST NOT BE READ AS RESOLVING IT.
  Both exits belong to an ADR owner: ADR-0023 EM-22's geometry grows to 77x39,
  or ADR-0007 §7.4's `binding` field — 219 of the 377 bytes — carries the
  TunnelKeyBinding by reference instead of by value. W-23 is the wave's standing
  lesson that a specified derivation is not an implementation agent's to
  improve. Until one of those lands, the contract is nameable and the default
  channel still does not fit it.

